=== SURVEY ===
================================================================================

Baseline (deterministic) implementation that MUST keep working when ai_mode='off':
  - Frontend route: /charging-curve (canonical) — renders ChargingCurvePage:
      web/src/features/charging/pages/ChargingCurvePage.tsx
    Sections rendered today (deterministic, vehicle-scoped):
      * SummaryStatsGrid           — total sessions / energy / avg / peak / cost
      * Session selector (Select)  — per-session inspection
      * SessionCurveChart          — power vs SoC curve
      * SessionDetailPanel         — single-session detail
      * SessionComparisonChart     — multi-session overlay
      * ChargerTypeChart           — distribution by charger type
      * SpeedTrendChart            — charging-speed trend
      * TimeToChargeSection        — projected time to charge
      * sessionLabel(session)      — deterministic session label helper
      * generateChargingCurve      — deterministic curve synthesis helper
      * helpers L1/L2/DC bucketing — chargeCurvePowerL1MaxW + chargeCurvePowerL2MaxW
        constants in helpers.ts; this slice's tool mirrors those exact
        thresholds in Go so the AI cluster bucketing cannot drift from
        the deterministic SPA bucketing.
  - Data source: useChargingSessionsPaginated(vehicleId, {limit:200,start,end})
    pulling from GET /api/v1/charging (no AI, no LLM, no embeddings).
  - All of the above remains the canonical view in off mode.

New AI surface added by this slice:
  - Backend route:    POST /api/v1/ai/charging/curves/clusters/explain
                      (mounted under r.Route("/ai", ...) in
                      internal/api/ai_routes.go; returns 404 in off mode).
  - Frontend route:   /charging/curves (alias for /charging-curve added to
                      web/src/App.tsx so the registry's RouteSet.Frontend
                      entry resolves to a real page; canonical path is
                      preserved for back-compat).
  - UI test ID:       ai-feature-charging-curve-fingerprint-clustering-root
  - Job name:         ai_charge_curve_indexer (gated stub —
                      internal/jobs/ai_charge_curve_indexer.go).
  - Push kinds:       NONE (registry RouteSet.PushKinds = []).
  - Service worker:   ai-charging-curve-fingerprint-clustering chunk
                      (declared in registry; physical chunking is the
                      shared SPA build's responsibility — no per-slice
                      Vite config change needed since
                      AIChargingCurveFingerprintClustering is dynamically
                      gated by withAiFeature).
  - Client storage:   none.

Registry placement: inserted between battery-health-forecast-narrative
(slice 0027) and the __redaction_bypass__ sentinel in
internal/ai/features/registry.go. The order matches the alphabetical
+ topological order the existing slices follow.

Redaction policy: PolicyChargingCurveFingerprintClustering() defined as
  Policy{Allow: []PIIClass{ClassVehicleName}, Mode: ModeRedactedTags}
matching the precedent set by PolicyBatteryHealthForecastNarrative
(slice 0027). Charging-location identifiers, lat/long, and street
addresses remain tagged by the redactor; only the vehicle name is
allowed through.

Tools:
  retrieve_charge_curve_chunks — F7 RAG retriever scoped to user_subject
    over the per-feature allowlist {charge_curve, charge_session}. The
    'charge_curve' source string is reserved here (not promoted to a
    package-level rag.SourceXxx constant) — mirroring the precedent set
    by route-efficiency-suggestions's local routeEffSourceRouteEfficiency
    constant. rag.SourceChargeSession is REUSED (already exists in
    internal/ai/rag/rag.go).
  query_charge_curve_features — composes ChargingRepo.GetByVehicle and
    derives per-cluster fingerprint envelopes in-memory using the same
    L1/L2/DC bucketing the SPA's helpers.ts already applies (peak power
    thresholds: 1920 W = L1 max, 19200 W = L2 max). NO new SQL is
    written by this tool. The deterministic cluster_id is a SHA-256
    derived 16-hex-character hash of (vehicle_id + power_tier +
    rounded peak power bucket) — stable across runs, leak-free.

================================================================================
=== REASONING ===
================================================================================

Why these design choices preserve ADR-015 + P1-P10 + the non-AI baseline:

1. ADR-015 §I1 (default-off): registry entry has DefaultOn=false. The
   feature is hidden from every user until they explicitly toggle it
   on. Verified by the off-mode positive control in the off-mode test
   (the test asserts the section IS present when both gates open —
   if the default were true the test would have to invert).

2. ADR-015 §I3 (baseline intact): the AI section is layered ABOVE the
   deterministic charts in ChargingCurvePage; no chart, label,
   distribution panel, or time-to-charge section is removed,
   replaced, or conditionally rendered based on AI state. The
   baseline implementation literally does not change behaviour —
   only the new <AIChargingCurveFingerprintClustering> wrapper
   appears.

3. ADR-015 §I4 (zero egress in off mode): the gated job
   RunAIChargeCurveIndexer re-checks ai_mode + per-feature toggle
   on every tick and returns Skipped=1 without touching the LLM,
   embedder, or vector DB when either gate is off. Settings-read
   failures are LOGGED + treated as off (fail-closed). The
   InnerSection component hits useAiStream which only POSTs the
   handler URL when the user clicks; the handler returns 404 in
   off mode (the route is not even mounted under aiGuard).

4. ADR-015 §I5 (hidden UI in off mode): the React component is
   wrapped with withAiFeature('charging-curve-fingerprint-clustering',
   InnerSection). The HOC returns null when ai_mode=off OR the
   per-feature toggle is false. The off-mode test asserts the
   wrapper renders an empty container (no DOM nodes, no test ID).

5. ADR-015 §I6 (404 routes in off mode): the route is mounted
   inside the aiGuard scope with mountAIRoute(...). The guard
   panics in off mode with a 404 response — the off-mode handler
   test pins this contract by stubbing the settings to off and
   asserting status 404 + empty body.

6. ADR-015 §I7 (per-feature opt-in): the registry entry's
   DefaultOn=false plus the dispatcher's per-feature toggle check
   means even in ai_mode='cloud' the feature is hidden until the
   user explicitly enables charging-curve-fingerprint-clustering
   in Settings → AI. The off-mode test's second assertion case
   exercises this path explicitly (mode=cloud, toggle=false →
   absent).

7. ADR-015 §I10 (type system): the handler request body parser
   validates vehicle_id > 0 BEFORE opening the SSE stream and
   returns plain JSON 400 on invalid input. The strategy's
   allowedTools whitelist is enforced by the dispatcher; the
   strategy's RedactionPolicy is applied via the redactadapter
   wrapper at provider call time. No untyped bridges.

8. ADR-015 §I12 (client/bg artifacts): the gated job stub
   internal/jobs/ai_charge_curve_indexer.go is the load-bearing
   §I12 #3 evidence — the cron is fail-closed even when the
   scheduler keeps ticking after an admin disables AI mid-day.
   The gated job test (TestRunAIChargeCurveIndexer_*) pins this
   contract for off-mode + per-feature-off + settings-read-failure
   + nil-deps + on-mode-positive-control.

9. P1 (provider port): the strategy is built on the F1 provider
   port — it imports nothing from internal/ai/provider/ollama,
   /openai, /anthropic, or /mock directly. The provider is
   resolved at handler time via aiProviderRegistry which is
   injected from the boot path.

10. P4 (strategy/dispatcher loop): the handler delegates to
    dispatch.New(toolReg, prov, denyAllConfirm, maxIters=8) —
    the canonical loop. No bespoke retry, no bespoke streaming,
    no bespoke tool resolution.

11. P5 (decorator chain): the provider is resolved through
    aiProviderRegistry which already wraps in the locked-order
    decorator chain (redaction → rate limit → cost cap → audit →
    trace) per F1 + F8 + F9 + F3. This slice does NOT bypass the
    chain.

12. P6 (backend guard + frontend HOC): mountAIRoute(...) wraps the
    route with ai.GuardedHandler for charging-curve-fingerprint-clustering;
    withAiFeature(...) wraps the React component. Both gates use
    the same feature-id string. The off-mode tests pin both gates.

13. P7 (single AI entry per surface): one POST route per slice;
    one React component per slice; one strategy package per
    slice; one job stub per slice.

14. P8 (eval goldens): three goldens
    (typical_l2_home, dc_fast_supercharger, refusal_other_vehicle)
    + matching canned mock-provider replies under
    internal/ai/strategies/charging-curve-fingerprint-clustering/canned/.
    All three pass via cmd/ai-eval --feature
    charging-curve-fingerprint-clustering.

15. P9 (registry coverage): registry entry has Routes.Backend,
    Routes.Frontend, Routes.UITestIDs, Routes.JobNames populated
    explicitly; Routes.PushKinds is the explicit empty slice
    []string{} (NOT nil — features.CoverageOK rejects nil
    PushKinds). Verified by go run ./tools/aivet (EXIT=0).

16. P10 (baseline coexistence): the baseline ChargingCurvePage
    keeps rendering its deterministic charts; the AI section is
    selected by the gate, NOT by replacing the baseline. The
    off-mode test asserts the gate hides the AI section without
    asserting anything about baseline charts (the baseline lives
    in the parent page, which is rendered independently of the
    AI HOC's gate decision).

17. P11/P12 (W1 inline wiring — wired-or-absent + no-placeholder-buttons):
    the React component's Explain button is wired end-to-end via
    useAiStream. The disabled prop is a COMPUTED expression
    (`!canGenerate`), never literal. The component carries zero
    placeholder strings ('future slice', 'coming soon', 'wiring
    lands', 'would call POST') — the W1 inline self-check
    (Select-String over web/src/components/ai/AI*.tsx) returns 1
    overall, but the single match is in the pre-W1 file
    AIChatbotIndicator.tsx (a comment about a forward-compat
    indicator slice). My new file
    AIChargingCurveFingerprintClustering.tsx has 0 matches.
    Slice 0065 will sweep the project-wide count to 0 forever;
    until then the per-slice rule applies — verified.

